diff --git a/docs/mac_floppy_bus.md b/docs/mac_floppy_bus.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9a52428
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/mac_floppy_bus.md
@@ -0,0 +1,103 @@
+# Macintosh floppy-port bus
+
+fujinet-nio can serve a 68000 Macintosh through its floppy port, the way the
+FujiNet Mac 68k board does with the classic firmware:
+
+* **HD20 disks** (Directly Connected Disks, DCD);
+* the **800K GCR floppy**;
+* **FujiBus from Mac programs**, carried in HD20 block I/O. This is new: no
+  firmware had it before.
+
+Source: `MacFloppyFramer` (`include/fujinet/io/transport/mac_floppy_framer.h`),
+`mac_gcr.{h,cpp}` (the FujiNet Mac firmware's GCR codec, unchanged), tests in
+`tests/test_mac_floppy_framer.cpp`.
+
+## Where it sits
+
+On the FujiNet Mac board a Raspberry Pi Pico is wired to the Mac's DB-19
+floppy connector. It runs the drive side of the DCD and floppy protocols in
+PIO, and talks to the FujiNet MCU over a 2 Mbaud UART with a one-character
+protocol. `MacFloppyFramer` is the FujiNet side of that UART protocol. It
+plugs into the ordinary `FujiBusTransport` as an `IFramer`:
+
+```
+Mac --IWM/DB-19--> drive side (Pico, or an emulator) --UART / TCP--> MacFloppyFramer
+                                                      |-- HD20 blocks   -> DiskService slots 1-4
+                                                      |-- floppy        -> DiskService slot 5
+                                                      `-- mailbox       -> FujiBusTransport -> devices
+```
+
+| Build | Channel | Serves |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `mac-floppy-tcp-debug` (POSIX preset) | TCP server (`channel.tcp_port`) | HD20, floppy, FujiBus: for emulators such as Snow |
+| `mac-floppy-fujimac-rev0` (ESP32-WROVER board) | UART2, 2 Mbaud, to the Pico | HD20 and FujiBus. The floppy waits for RMT track streaming |
+
+Slots use the FujiBus DiskService numbering. Slots 1-4 are DCD units 0-3 (the
+chain is the contiguous run of mounted slots from 1). Slot 5 is the floppy:
+a 400K or 800K sector image.
+
+## Drive side <-> FujiNet
+
+| From the drive side | Reply | |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `A`..`D` | none | select DCD unit 0..3 |
+| `R` b2 b1 b0 | 512 bytes | read block (24-bit, big endian) |
+| `W` b2 b1 b0 + 512 bytes | `w` / `e` | write block |
+| `T` | 336 bytes | HD20 status block (payload offsets 6..341) |
+| `0` / `4` | none | floppy step direction: in / out |
+| `1` | track \| 0x80, or `N` | floppy step |
+| `2` / `6` | `M` / `F` | floppy motor on / off |
+| `7` | `E` | the Mac ejected the floppy (the slot is unmounted and forgotten) |
+| `?` | `h` n (see `!`) | how many DCD units (fujinet-nio) |
+| `#` cyl side | nbits (u32 BE), bits | one GCR track, MSB first (emulators) |
+| `P` cyl side nbits bits | `p` n | a whole written track; changed sectors are written (emulators) |
+| `!` | none | polled mode (emulators) |
+
+Unsolicited, from the FujiNet: `h` n (DCD units), `s`/`d` t (400K/800K
+floppy in, head at t\|0x80), `u`/`l` (writable/locked), `r` (floppy
+removed).
+
+**Polled mode.** An unsolicited byte can land in front of a reply the drive
+side is already waiting for. The Pico avoids this by draining its UART before
+each command. An emulator on a socket sends `!` instead: after that NIO
+sends nothing unasked, and `?` answers `h` n f. Here f is the floppy: 0
+none, 1 400K, 2 800K, plus 0x80 if it is write protected.
+
+**The floppy.** On the board the ESP32 streams encoded tracks from its RMT
+peripheral, and receives the Mac's writes as captured WR bitstreams. The
+emulator commands replace those wires. `#` returns a track encoded from the
+sector image. `P` takes a track as the Mac left it. NIO searches it for
+address and data fields at every bit offset, twice around the track so a
+field written across the index is found too. It writes each good sector that
+differs from the image.
+
+## FujiBus through the mailbox
+
+Every HD20 unit reports 16 more blocks than its image holds. Those blocks
+never reach the image.
+
+* A **write** starting at the first of them carries one raw (un-SLIPped)
+  FujiBus packet.
+* **Reading** them returns the response.
+
+```
+block 0, bytes 0..15:  "FNIO"  version=1  state  seq (u16 BE)  length (u32 BE)  reserved (4)
+bytes 16..             the packet, continuing into blocks 1..15 (8176 bytes max)
+state:                 0 idle, 1 response ready, 2 request pending
+```
+
+The request's seq comes back with the response. A client polls block 0 until
+the state is 1 and the seq matches, then reads any further blocks.
+
+The Mac needs no driver, INIT or patch. It finds the mailbox by walking the
+drive queue and reading the block at `drive size - 16` for `FNIO`. It then
+uses driver-level `PBWrite`/`PBRead` on the ROM's `.Sony` driver, which
+handles HD20s on a 512Ke, Plus, SE and later. The board's Pico sees only
+ordinary block reads and writes. fujinet-nio-lib's `mac68k` target implements
+this (`src/platform/mac68k/fn_transport.c`), so Mac programs use the usual
+`fn_*` API.
+
+Limits:
+* HD20 images must stay at or below 65,519 blocks (32 MB less the mailbox);
+  the Mac rejects HD20s over 65,535 blocks.
+* A response larger than the mailbox is dropped.
